  <main class="read"><div class="ab">
    <div>
      <p>I'm a <strong>data analyst</strong> who treats analysis as the last mile before a decision. Across seven projects I've worked end to end — quantitative risk modelling, NLP, fraud detection, data governance, model monitoring, and a full-stack finance app — and written each one up the way I'd present to a stakeholder: <strong>the finding first, the methodology second.</strong></p>
      <p>What pulls me into this work is the point where a model becomes a recommendation — the threshold that turns into dollars a day, the drift signal that buys two weeks of warning, the VaR number that changes how a portfolio is weighted. I care as much about communicating that clearly as about getting it right.</p>
      <p>I'm pursuing a <strong>B.S. in Computational and Data Sciences at George Mason University</strong>, graduating in 2027. My coursework includes databases, statistics, scientific computing, cloud and distributed systems, natural language processing, data mining, and visualization.</p>
      <p>As an <strong>Event Assistant with George Mason University Student Centers</strong>, I turned two years of AV inventory records into an operational analysis of loss patterns, room risk, and inventory controls. That work strengthened my ability to explain technical findings to supervisors and stakeholders. I also bring team leadership experience from serving as President and VP of Programming for Beta Theta Pi.</p>
      <p>I'm looking for an entry-level <strong>data analyst, business intelligence, or operations analyst</strong> role where careful analysis leads to practical decisions. If that sounds like your team, let's talk.</p>
